(68, 'Hotel', 'Absheron + Outdoor Go Karting', 'Private Transfer', NULL, NULL, NULL, '10:00 – Leaving Baku to Absheron (1 way x 30 mins)\n\n 10:30 – Visit to Fire Temple (Ateshgah)\n - An ancient Zoroastrian site where flames were historically kept burning as part of religious rituals. The temple provides insight into Azerbaijan’s early religious history and Zoroastrian culture. Visitors can explore the site and learn about the traditions of fire worship.\n 11.30 - Burning Mountains (Yanardag)\n - A natural gas hillside that has been burning continuously for decades, creating a unique “burning mountain” phenomenon. It is a famous landmark of Azerbaijan, offering visitors spectacular views and a memorable experience of the country’s natural fiery landscape.\n\n 12:30 – Leaving Absheron to Baku\n\n 13:00 – Lunch Break\n\n 14:00 – Outdoor Go Karting Experience\n\n • Experience high-speed racing on an open-air karting track.\n\n • Safety briefing and equipment provided before the race.\n\n • Enjoy fresh air, wide curves, and longer straights for an exciting outdoor karting session.\n\n • Opportunity for timed laps, friendly competitions, and photos at the track.\n\n 16:00 – Panoramic City Tour of Baku\n\n • Drive through and stop at Baku Boulevard, Fountain Square, Flame Towers, and National Flag Square.\n\n • Enjoy panoramic views of the Caspian Sea, the city skyline, and architectural highlights.\n\n • Photo stops at landmarks and opportunity to learn about Baku’s history and modern development.\n\n 18:00 – Dinner Break (if included)\n\n 19:00 –Drop to Hotel\n\n Overnight in Baku', 41.00, 47.00, 47.00, 59.00, 118.00, 147.00, 177.00, 206.00, 206.00, 20.00, 47.00),
(69, 'Hotel', 'Old City Tour + Nizami Street', 'Private Transfer', NULL, NULL, NULL, '10:00 – Leaving Hotel to Old City \n\n- Old City tour starts from Shamakhi Gates followed by below places (Photo stop):\n - Shamakhi Gates – The historic entrance to the Old City, showcasing iconic stone architecture.\n - Sculptures of Azim Azimzadeh – Artistic monuments honoring Azerbaijan’s famed satirical painter and graphic artist.\n - Caravanserai of Bukhara – A restored medieval inn once used by Silk Road traders for rest and trade.\n - Caravanserai of Multani – A traditional guesthouse built for merchants from Multan, reflecting oriental caravan culture.\n - Maiden’s Tower – Baku’s mysterious UNESCO-listed tower offering panoramic views of the Old City.\n - Bazar Square – A lively historic square once serving as the commercial heart of Icherisheher.\n - Miniature Books’ Museum – A unique museum displaying one of the world’s largest collections of tiny books.\n - Shirvanshahs’ Palace – A majestic 15th-century royal complex showcasing medieval Azerbaijani architecture. \n\n 13:00 – Lunch Break\n\n 14:00 – Nizami Street Walking Tour \n • One of the city’s main pedestrian streets known for shopping, cafés, and historic architecture.\n • Popular walking area with boutiques, souvenir shops, and local restaurants.\n\n 18:00 – Dinner Break (if included)\n\n 19:00 –Drop to Hotel\n\n Overnight in Baku', 35.00, 38.00, 40.00, 50.00, 88.00, 147.00, 177.00, 206.00, 206.00, 20.00, 47.00),
(70, 'Hotel', 'Old City Tour + Boulevard Tour', 'Private Transfer', NULL, NULL, NULL, '10:00 – Leaving Hotel to Old City \n - Old City tour starts from Shamakhi Gates followed by below places (Photo stop):\n - Shamakhi Gates – The historic entrance to the Old City, showcasing iconic stone architecture.\n - Sculptures of Azim Azimzadeh – Artistic monuments honoring Azerbaijan’s famed satirical painter and graphic artist.\n - Caravanserai of Bukhara – A restored medieval inn once used by Silk Road traders for rest and trade.\n - Caravanserai of Multani – A traditional guesthouse built for merchants from Multan, reflecting oriental caravan culture.\n - Maiden’s Tower – Baku’s mysterious UNESCO-listed tower offering panoramic views of the Old City.\n - Bazar Square – A lively historic square once serving as the commercial heart of Icherisheher.\n - Miniature Books’ Museum – A unique museum displaying one of the world’s largest collections of tiny books.\n - Shirvanshahs’ Palace – A majestic 15th-century royal complex showcasing medieval Azerbaijani architecture. \n\n13:00 – Lunch Break\n\n 14:00 – Baku boulevard tour starts along the Seaside (Photostop) followed by: \n -Carpet Museum \n -Mini Venice of Baku\n -Deniz Mall \n -Ferries Wheel \n -Carpet Museums\n -Cristal Hall\n \n 18:00 – Dinner Break (if included)\n \n19:00 –Drop to Hotel\n \nOvernight in Baku', 35.00, 38.00, 40.00, 50.00, 88.00, 147.00, 177.00, 206.00, 206.00, 20.00, 47.00),
(71, 'Hotel', 'Old City Tour + Panaromic City Tour', 'Private Transfer', NULL, NULL, NULL, '10:00 – Leaving Hotel to Old City \n - Old City tour starts from Shamakhi Gates followed by below places (Photo stop):\n - Shamakhi Gates – The historic entrance to the Old City, showcasing iconic stone architecture.\n - Sculptures of Azim Azimzadeh – Artistic monuments honoring Azerbaijan’s famed satirical painter and graphic artist.\n - Caravanserai of Bukhara – A restored medieval inn once used by Silk Road traders for rest and trade.\n - Caravanserai of Multani – A traditional guesthouse built for merchants from Multan, reflecting oriental caravan culture.\n - Maiden’s Tower – Baku’s mysterious UNESCO-listed tower offering panoramic views of the Old City.\n - Bazar Square – A lively historic square once serving as the commercial heart of Icherisheher.\n - Miniature Books’ Museum – A unique museum displaying one of the world’s largest collections of tiny books.\n - Shirvanshahs’ Palace – A majestic 15th-century royal complex showcasing medieval Azerbaijani architecture. \n\n13:00 – Lunch Break\n\n 14:00 – Evening City Tour starts with panoramic full evening city view of Baku followed by:\n\n - Highland Park (Which is the highest point in the Azerbaijani capital, opening a panoramic view of the city and its bay. The city\'s residents and visitors often come here to see a bird\'s eye view of Baku, and just have a walk. The Upland Park can be reached by cable railway (funicular) in 7-8 minutes from the boulevard or by climbing up the endless stone stairs.)\nFlame Towers (The city\'s tallest building. The height of 182 m. Flame Towers - consisting of three high-rise buildings in the complex.)\n \n18:00 – Dinner Break (if included)\n \n19:00 –Drop to Hotel\n \nOvernight in Baku', 35.00, 38.00, 40.00, 50.00, 88.00, 147.00, 177.00, 206.00, 206.00, 20.00, 47.00),
(72, 'Hotel', 'Old City Tour + Heydar Aliyev Centre', 'Private Transfer', NULL, NULL, NULL, '10:00 – Leaving Hotel to Old City \n  - Old City tour starts from Shamakhi Gates followed by below places (Photo stop):\n - Shamakhi Gates – The historic entrance to the Old City, showcasing iconic stone architecture.\n - Sculptures of Azim Azimzadeh – Artistic monuments honoring Azerbaijan’s famed satirical painter and graphic artist.\n - Caravanserai of Bukhara – A restored medieval inn once used by Silk Road traders for rest and trade.\n - Caravanserai of Multani – A traditional guesthouse built for merchants from Multan, reflecting oriental caravan culture.\n - Maiden’s Tower – Baku’s mysterious UNESCO-listed tower offering panoramic views of the Old City.\n - Bazar Square – A lively historic square once serving as the commercial heart of Icherisheher.\n - Miniature Books’ Museum – A unique museum displaying one of the world’s largest collections of tiny books.\n - Shirvanshahs’ Palace – A majestic 15th-century royal complex showcasing medieval Azerbaijani architecture. \n\n13:00 – Lunch Break\n \n14:00 – Visit the Heydar Aliyev Centre\n - Visit to Heydar Aliyev Center & “I LOVE BAKU” sign Photostop\n - If it’s first time of your arrival to Baku, just be aware of that \n from the bird view you can clearly see the Heydar Aliyev’s \n Signature on the Heydar Aliyev’s Center design. It’s a wave-like\n building rises from a bed of concrete. Its immobile white walls \n look as though they have been frozen mid-ripple. The abstract \n architecture is unlike anything else in the capital city. The \n Heydar Aliyev Center, which was designed by renowned architect Zaha Hadid.\n \n18:00 – Dinner Break (if included)\n \n19:00 –Drop to Hotel\n \nOvernight in Baku', 35.00, 38.00, 40.00, 50.00, 88.00, 147.00, 177.00, 206.00, 206.00, 20.00, 47.00),
